Nothing happened to number [1].  There is no number [1].  If you were
to visit #nethack on irc.freenode.net and type ??ideas, you would be
presented with a list of ideas from Rodney the resident bot.  The first
item on the list is actually a placeholder.  It typically says
something to the effect of "Please add your YANIs to this list."  The
subsequent items in the list are the ideas.  Note that ideas often
reference one another like, "[7]: This idea is an extension of idea
[3]."  When I post the ideas to RGRN, the entire post was created from
the log files of my IRC client by a script.  I do this in the interest
of saving time, as it would certainly take a long time to manipulatate
large lists of ideas by hand (and I would know!  I didn't always have
my little script).  As you can see, when the ideas are posted to RGRN,
the first item in the list is no longer appropriate.  But renumbering
all of the other ideas in the list so that they begin with [1] would
cause that hypothetical idea [7] above (now called idea [6]) to claim
to be an extension of what used to be idea [4].  Rather than carefully
edit the list by hand to update these cross-references, I generally
just lop off idea [1] and place the last idea where [1] had been.  That
way I only have to renumber one idea.  Sometimes I forget to do this,
or it is inconvenient for some reason.  In this case, I think I was
merely being lazy.

